      en: "Sungai Kebun is a historic water village in Bandar Seri Begawan, Brunei, known for its stilt houses along the Brunei River. Established in the early 20th century, it served as a trading post for local produce. The village covers approximately 0.5 km² and has a population of around 2,500. Its economy relies on fishing and small-scale commerce. The architecture blends traditional Malay design with modern materials, featuring wooden walkways. The community maintains a strong cultural identity, with waterways as primary thoroughfares. Recent urban development has improved sanitation and transport links, yet the village retains its unique aquatic character. Ecotourism initiatives promote sustainable visits to the stilt settlements.",
